  <h2>How we eat</h2>
  <p>
    We don't prescribe a dietary ideology. We prescribe a standard and a structure:
    <strong>what</strong> enters the house, <strong>when</strong> it gets eaten, and
    <strong>what goes first</strong>. Three rules, learnable in a week, sustainable for life —
    because the goal isn't a diet phase. The goal is the best mind possible until your last day of life,
    and every meal is either a deposit toward that or a tax against it.
  </p>

  <h2>1 · What you eat — the sourcing standard</h2>
  <p>
    Modern food is calorie-dense and nutrient-sparse by design. The same protein gram from a
    grass-finished ribeye and a feedlot chicken breast carry meaningfully different inflammatory,
    hormonal, and micronutrient profiles. So the first rule isn't about macros — it's about what
    qualifies as food.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Animals raised on their natural diet</h3>
      <p>Grass-finished beef, pasture-raised pork and poultry, wild-caught seafood. The baseline criteria for every protein on the plate.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Real food first</h3>
      <p>Single-ingredient foods, minimally processed. If it has a mascot or a health claim on the box, it isn't on the plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Organ meat integration</h3>
      <p>Liver, heart, and kidney are the most nutrient-dense foods available — whole, or encapsulated for those who won't eat them from the pan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Zero seed oils</h3>
      <p>Canola, soybean, sunflower, safflower, and corn oils drive linoleic-acid accumulation and chronic inflammation. They're removed entirely — not moderated.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>2 · When you eat — the eating window</h2>
  <p>
    The default 4M window is <strong>9am to 6pm, five days a week</strong>. Morning training or a fasted
    walk happens <em>before</em> the window opens — you move first, then you eat. Give yourself up to two
    off-days a week for what matters — breakfast with the kids or grandkids — then walk 30 minutes after
    the meal. Men optimizing sleep and evening cortisol can tighten the close to 4–5pm. What matters most
    isn't perfection; it's that most days eating has a start, an end, and nothing after the end.
    Late-night food is one of the most reliable ways to degrade deep sleep — and deep sleep is when the
    brain does its maintenance.
  </p>

  <aside class="protein-first-rule">
    <span class="protein-first-tag">Universal Rule</span>
    <h2>3 · Protein first — 30–40g breaks the fast</h2>
    <p><strong>After your fasted morning training or walk, the first thing you eat is 30–40g of lean protein.</strong> Whatever else the day holds, the window opens with protein — every day, no exceptions.</p>
    <p>The threshold isn't arbitrary — it's the leucine signal that triggers maximal muscle protein synthesis in adult men, which matters more every year after 40 as sarcopenia works against you. On GLP-1 protocols where total intake drops sharply, this rule is even more critical: it protects the lean mass that drives metabolic flexibility and visceral-fat reduction. Daily target: roughly 1g of protein per pound of ideal bodyweight.</p>
    <p class="protein-first-examples"><em>What 30–40g of lean protein looks like:</em> 6 oz chicken or turkey breast · 5–6 oz lean grass-finished beef · 6 oz wild salmon · 6 eggs · 1.5–2 cups high-protein Greek yogurt · 1 scoop whey isolate with cottage cheese · 6 oz wild tuna.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>Eliminate the insulting behavior</h2>
  <p>
    Most men don't need to add anything to fix their nutrition — they need to stop insulting their
    biology at the table. The big four: seed oils in everything fried or packaged, eating within three
    hours of bed, liquid calories that spike and crash, and grazing all day so insulin never rests.
    Eliminate those and the sourcing standard above does the rest.
  </p>
  <p>Inflammatory food is a MindSpan tax paid at every meal. The three rules on this page are among the simplest levers a man has for moving his score in the right direction — starting at the very next meal.</p>

